---
// Coding agents, as a how-to: install one, point it at the machine, hand it a
// problem, give it a local model, and sandbox it. Every command and message is
// cli/pulsar's (agent_*, sandbox_*, model_*), scripts/pulsar-mcp's,
// scripts/pulsar-agents' (the Agents app: its labels and shortcuts), or
// system_files/etc/profile.d/pulsar-agents.sh's. The transcripts are real
// runs. What an agent can and cannot reach is docs/AGENTS-SAFETY.md, which
// this page links to rather than restates.
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import { APP_ID, ORG_ID } from '../../data/site';

const DESCRIPTION =
  'How to install Claude Code, Codex, Antigravity CLI or opencode on Pulsar, point one at a crash or a local model, and leave it running in a session you can undo.';

const LIST = [
  '$ pulsar agent list',
  'NAME      COMMAND   INSTALL                            STATE      DESCRIPTION',
  'claude    claude    npm @anthropic-ai/claude-code      -          Claude Code (Anthropic)',
  'codex     codex     npm @openai/codex                  -          Codex CLI (OpenAI)',
  'agy       agy       download antigravity.google        -          Antigravity CLI (Google)',
  'opencode  opencode  npm @opencode/cli                  -          opencode (open source, any provider)',
  '',
  'none is installed by default. toolbox: agents  prefix: /var/home/you/.local/share/pulsar/agents',
].join('\n');

const ADD = [
  'pulsar agent add claude       # then type `claude` in any terminal',
  'pulsar agent remove claude    # its command and package; your logins stay',
].join('\n');

const STATUS = [
  '$ pulsar agent',
  'guide    /usr/share/pulsar/AGENTS.md  (pulsar agent guide)',
  'agent    claude    native',
  'sandbox  off here (default)  (pulsar agent sandbox)',
  'guard    off: layering and system Flatpak installs do not prompt  (sudo pulsar agent guard on)',
].join('\n');

const ASK = [
  'pulsar agent default claude                      # the agent ask starts',
  'pulsar agent ask                                 # "what, if anything, looks wrong?"',
  'pulsar agent ask why does the fan never stop     # your own question',
  'pulsar agent ask --crash latest                  # the newest crash, explained',
  'pulsar agent ask --with codex                    # someone other than the default',
].join('\n');

const SESSIONS = [
  'pulsar agent run claude --tmux work              # start claude in a session named work',
  'pulsar agent run claude --tmux work --worktree   # the same, in its own git worktree',
  'pulsar agent run claude --tmux work --prompt "fix the flaky test"',
  'pulsar agent attach work                         # back onto work, from any terminal',
  'pulsar agent attach                              # back onto the one you were on last',
  'pulsar agent sessions                            # every session, and what each is doing',
  'pulsar agent usage work                          # its model, context and tokens (Claude Code)',
  'pulsar agent diff work                           # what it changed since it started',
  'pulsar agent sessions --archive work             # done with it; --unarchive brings it back',
].join('\n');

const UNDO = [
  'pulsar agent undo work --dry-run                 # what an undo would change',
  'pulsar agent undo work                           # the files as they were when work started',
  'pulsar agent undo work --redo                    # take the undo back',
  'pulsar agent undo work --file src/app.ts         # one file, even while work runs',
].join('\n');

const DISK = [
  'pulsar agent disk                                # free space on /var, and the big things in it',
  'pulsar agent disk --delete <path>                # one of the things it listed',
  'pulsar doctor suspend-room                       # NVIDIA image: whether a suspend will fit',
].join('\n');

// cli/pulsar agent_read, agent_send, agent_inbox, agent_messages and cmd_job,
// as on OS main 1e7f098e.
const MESSAGES = [
  'pulsar agent read work                           # its screen, as it is now',
  'pulsar agent send work "the API tests pass now"  # into its prompt, at the end of its turn',
  'pulsar agent inbox work                          # its messages both ways, each with an id',
  'pulsar agent send work --reply-to <id> "on it"   # answer one',
  'pulsar agent messages off                        # or on, with --cap N',
].join('\n');

const JOBS = [
  'pulsar job run --name check -- make check        # its own tmux, a log, a notification',
  'pulsar job run --notify work -- make check       # and the session work hears how it ended',
  'pulsar job log check                             # its output',
].join('\n');

// cli/pulsar cmd_machine and agent_send, as on OS main 0d66ec30.
const MACHINE = [
  'pulsar machine add desktop you@desktop.local     # checks Pulsar answers there, then saves it',
  'pulsar machine check                             # each one’s release, GPU and agents',
  'pulsar machine sessions                          # every machine’s agent sessions',
  'pulsar machine run desktop claude --name train --prompt "run the training here"',
  'pulsar machine job desktop --notify work -- python train.py',
  'pulsar machine fetch desktop train               # its branch back, as desktop/train',
  'pulsar agent send train@desktop "the data is in ~/data"',
].join('\n');

const SSH_SETUP = [
  'ssh you@desktop.local          # once, to accept its host key',
  'ssh-copy-id you@desktop.local  # your key, so Pulsar never has to ask',
].join('\n');

const MODEL = [
  'pulsar agent model on                            # the default: Qwen2.5 Coder 7B',
  'pulsar agent model on --model <owner/repo[:quant]> --at-login',
  'pulsar agent model                               # state, endpoint, key, the aider line',
  'pulsar agent model off --purge                   # stop and remove it, and delete the models',
].join('\n');

const SANDBOX = [
  'pulsar agent sandbox on               # every project, from now on',
  'pulsar agent sandbox on --here        # just this one',
  'pulsar agent sandbox                  # what applies here, and why',
  'pulsar agent sandbox push branches    # never the default branch',
  'pulsar agent sandbox push off         # no pushes, no pull requests',
].join('\n');

const SANDBOX_START = [
  '$ claude',
  'sandboxed: only /var/home/you/code/app is visible; push on through the gate',
].join('\n');

const SANDBOX_MORE = [
  'pulsar agent sandbox allow ~/.local/bin/my-hook     # one more path in, read-only',
  'pulsar agent sandbox allow ~/notes --rw --here      # read-write, this project only',
  'pulsar agent sandbox repin                          # after you really changed a remote',
  'pulsar agent run claude --no-sandbox                # one session outside it',
].join('\n');

const REPO_TOML = ['# .pulsar/agent.toml, committed to the repo', 'sandbox = "on"', 'push = "branches"'].join('\n');

const GUARD = [
  '$ pulsar agent guard',
  'guard: off',
  '',
  '  org.projectatomic.rpmostree1.install-uninstall-packages    no prompt',
  '  org.projectatomic.rpmostree1.rollback                      no prompt',
  '  org.projectatomic.rpmostree1.cleanup                       no prompt',
  '  org.freedesktop.Flatpak.app-install                        no prompt',
  '  org.freedesktop.Flatpak.app-uninstall                      no prompt',
].join('\n');

// cli/pulsar agent_context_path, register_mcp, agent_skills_path.
const WIRING: [string, string, string, string][] = [
  ['Claude Code', '~/.claude/rules/pulsar.md', '~/.claude.json', '~/.claude/skills'],
  ['Codex', '~/.codex/AGENTS.md', '~/.codex/config.toml', '~/.agents/skills'],
  ['Antigravity CLI', '~/.gemini/config/rules/pulsar.md', '~/.gemini/config/mcp_config.json', '~/.gemini/config/skills'],
  ['opencode', '~/.config/opencode/AGENTS.md', '~/.config/opencode/opencode.json', '~/.config/opencode/skills'],
];

// scripts/pulsar-mcp's TOOLS, as on OS main 1e7f098e. The third column is
// its readOnly flag turned around: the tools an agent is told change
// something, so it asks you first.
const TOOLS: [string, string, boolean][] = [
  ['doctor', 'the health checks, all or one', false],
  ['status', 'deployments, pins and layered packages', false],
  ['manifest', 'the image, the variant, the kernel and the hardware', false],
  ['report', 'the redacted diagnosis report, with a crash if asked', false],
  ['crashes', 'your crashes this boot', false],
  ['update_check', 'whether a newer image is out', false],
  ['agent_status', 'the guide, the agents, guard, the pin and the sandbox setting', false],
  ['theme_list, theme_current', 'the desktop themes', false],
  ['theme_set', 'sets your desktop theme (pulsar theme revert undoes it)', true],
  ['sessions', 'the agent sessions, and what each is doing', false],
  ['session_read', 'another session’s screen', false],
  ['session_message', 'a message to another session, or a reply', true],
  ['inbox', 'a session’s messages, both ways', false],
  ['job_start', 'starts a long command as a job', true],
  ['jobs, job_log', 'the jobs, and one’s output', false],
  ['job_stop', 'stops a job', true],
  ['machines', 'your other Pulsar machines', false],
  ['machine_run, machine_job', 'starts an agent or a job on another machine, with your branch', true],
  ['machine_fetch', 'brings a session’s branch back from another machine', true],
];

const JSONLD = [
  {
    '@type': 'TechArticle',
    headline: 'Coding agents on Pulsar',
    description: DESCRIPTION,
    about: { '@id': APP_ID },
    author: { '@id': ORG_ID },
    publisher: { '@id': ORG_ID },
    url: new URL('/docs/agents', Astro.site).href,
  },
];
const TOC = [
  { id: 'install', label: 'Install one', level: 2 },
  { id: 'briefing', label: 'What it knows about the machine', level: 2 },
  { id: 'ask', label: 'Ask it to fix something', level: 2 },
  { id: 'app', label: 'The Agents app', level: 2 },
  { id: 'sessions', label: 'Sessions from a terminal', level: 2 },
  { id: 'disk', label: 'Disk space', level: 2 },
  { id: 'messages', label: 'Sessions talking to each other', level: 2 },
  { id: 'machines', label: 'Your other machines', level: 2 },
  { id: 'model', label: 'A model on your own GPU', level: 2 },
  { id: 'sandbox', label: 'The sandbox', level: 2 },
  { id: 'guard', label: 'Guard and checkpoints', level: 2 },
];
---

<Docs
  title="Coding agents"
  fullTitle="Coding agents on Pulsar | Pulsar docs"
  description={DESCRIPTION}
  path="/docs/agents"
  headings={TOC}
  jsonLd={JSONLD}
>
  <h1>Coding agents</h1>
  <p class="intro">
    Pulsar doesn’t come with a coding agent. It can install Claude Code, Codex, Antigravity CLI or
    opencode for you, each in a toolbox.
  </p>

  <h2 id="install">Install one</h2>
  <p>The welcome window offers them at first login, or you can add one from a terminal.</p>
  <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" filename="pulsar agent list" code={LIST}></arc-code-block>
  <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" language="bash" filename="add" code={ADD}></arc-code-block>
  <p>
    <code>add</code> creates an <code>agents</code> toolbox, installs the agent into{' '}
    <code>~/.local/share/pulsar/agents</code>, and adds a command to <code>~/.local/bin</code>.
    Claude Code, Codex and opencode come from npm, and Node is installed in the toolbox with the
    toolbox’s own sudo, which asks for no password. Antigravity CLI has no package, so{' '}
    <code>add</code> reads Google’s release manifest and checks the download’s SHA-512, as Google’s
    own <code>install.sh</code> does. opencode is version 2 (<code>@opencode/cli</code>), and{' '}
    <code>add</code> removes a 1.x it installed earlier. It layers nothing on the host. An agent you
    installed yourself is left in place (listed as <code>native</code>) and still gets the setup
    below.
  </p>
  <p>
    Gemini CLI and aider aren’t listed any more, but <code>add</code>, <code>run</code> and{' '}
    <code>remove</code> still work for them, and <code>add</code> first says why they were dropped:
    Gemini CLI no longer signs in free or Google AI Pro/Ultra accounts, and aider has had no release
    since February 2026.
  </p>
  <arc-alert variant="info" heading="The toolbox shares your home folder">
    The agents live in your home folder, so <code>toolbox rm -f agents</code> removes Node and
    leaves them. <code>pulsar agent remove</code> takes one off. An agent in the toolbox can read
    your whole home folder and use your desktop session, as you can. To keep it to one project, use{' '}
    <a href="#sandbox">the sandbox</a>.
  </arc-alert>

  <h2 id="briefing">What it knows about the machine</h2>
  <p>
    The briefing is <code>/usr/share/pulsar/AGENTS.md</code>. It tells an agent that{' '}
    <code>/usr</code> is read-only, that dev tools go in a toolbox and apps in Flatpaks, what a
    rollback covers, and what it must never do. <code>pulsar agent guide</code> prints it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>add</code> puts the guide in each agent’s own instructions, unless a file is already there, and
    links the image’s skills (including one that makes themes). It registers{' '}
    <code>pulsar mcp</code> only if the agent has no <code>pulsar</code> server yet.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Agent</th><th>Guide</th><th>MCP server</th><th>Skills</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {WIRING.map(([agent, guide, mcp, skills]) => (
        <tr><td>{agent}</td><td><code>{guide}</code></td><td><code>{mcp}</code></td><td><code>{skills}</code></td></tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    Antigravity CLI ignores a rules file without a <code>trigger</code>, so its{' '}
    <code>pulsar.md</code> is a five-line file set to <code>trigger: always_on</code> that includes
    the guide. The others get a link. The MCP server answers with these tools:
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>What it answers</th><th>Changes something</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {TOOLS.map(([t, what, write]) => (
        <tr><td><code>{t}</code></td><td>{what}</td><td>{write ? 'yes' : ''}</td></tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>
    The tools that change something are marked as writes, so the agent asks you before using
    them. A sandboxed agent has no <code>pulsar mcp</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>pulsar agent</code> on its own shows how it’s set up.
  </p>
  <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" filename="pulsar agent" code={STATUS}></arc-code-block>

  <h2 id="ask">Ask it to fix something</h2>
  <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" language="bash" filename="ask" code={ASK}></arc-code-block>
  <p>
    <code>ask</code> writes a redacted <code>pulsar report</code> (plus the crash, if named) to a
    private file in <code>$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR</code> (deleted at logout), then starts your agent with
    a prompt pointing at it, your question, and “Do not change the system without asking me
    first.” opencode sends it once a model is set up.
  </p>
  <p>
    A crash notification’s <strong>Ask</strong> button runs <code>pulsar agent ask --crash</code>{' '}
    in a new terminal. The report has the stack trace and logs, never the core file{' '}
    (<a href="/docs/troubleshooting#crashes">Troubleshooting</a>). The agent sends what it reads to
    its provider (Anthropic, OpenAI, Google, or the one you set up in opencode).
  </p>

  <h2 id="app">The Agents app</h2>
  <p>
    Agents, in the app grid, lists every session under its project, with the ones that need you
    first. A session is the agent’s own terminal, inside the window. Closing the window leaves the
    agents working.
  </p>
  {/* assets-static/agents-app.webp, from the OS session's 1800x1133 capture
      (pulsar-agents-in-use.png), chosen by Nick on 2026-11-20. */}
  <figure class="shot">
    <a href="/assets/agents-app.webp">
      <img
        src="/assets/agents-app.webp"
        alt="The Agents app: sessions grouped by project on the left, a Claude Code session in the middle explaining its fix for a flaky login test, and Changes on the right with the diff of src/auth/login.py."
        width="1800"
        height="1133"
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />
    </a>
  </figure>
  <ul>
    <li>
      With no session open, the window asks “What should the agent do?” What you type becomes the
      agent’s first message, and the session’s name unless you fill in Name. The agent, a worktree
      of its own and the sandbox are picked beside it, and remembered per project.
    </li>
    <li>
      Opening a stopped session continues it. Claude Code and opencode pick up the same
      conversation, and Antigravity CLI does once Pulsar has seen its conversation. Codex picks up
      its latest conversation in that folder.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Changes</strong> (Ctrl+Shift+D) lists the files the agent changed since the session
      started, or since the last commit, with each file’s diff and <strong>Revert File</strong>. A
      comment on a diff line is typed into the agent’s prompt as <code>path:line: …</code> for you
      to send.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>History</strong> lists the snapshot taken at each start. <strong>Go Back</strong> puts
      the files back, stopping the agent first if it’s working, and the Undo after it takes that
      back.
    </li>
    <li>
      The bar under a session shows the model, what the agent is doing, how full its context is,
      Codex’s 5-hour and weekly limits, the sandbox, the branch and how many files changed. Claude
      Code and Antigravity CLI don’t write their plan limits to disk, so they show context only.
    </li>
    <li>
      Archive (the × on a session, or Ctrl+Shift+Delete) hides it, with Undo. The app deletes
      archived sessions after a week, with their snapshots and scratch folders. Preferences
      (Ctrl+,) sets that to 1 day, 30 days or never. A worktree with
      uncommitted changes is kept, and so is every branch.
    </li>
    <li>Ctrl+Shift+B narrows the list to the agents’ tiles.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    In the app a session has no tmux keys, menus or status line, and when the agent quits you get{' '}
    <strong>Continue</strong>. A terminal attached to the same session keeps tmux. A notification’s{' '}
    <strong>Open</strong>, Pulsar Commands and the Agents page in Pulsar Settings all open the
    session in the app.
  </p>

  <h2 id="sessions">Sessions from a terminal</h2>
  <p>
    <code>pulsar agent run &lt;agent&gt; --tmux &lt;name&gt;</code> starts the agent in a named tmux
    session that outlives its terminal window, or attaches to it if it’s already running. Sessions
    run on a tmux server of their own (<code>-L pulsar-agents</code>), apart from yours. tmux is in
    the image. The app shows these sessions too.
  </p>
  <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" language="bash" filename="sessions" code={SESSIONS}></arc-code-block>
  <p>
    A session that ended starts again with its agent, folder, conversation and sandbox setting.{' '}
    <code>--new</code> starts a fresh conversation. With <code>--worktree</code>, the session gets a
    branch named after it, checked out in <code>&lt;repo&gt;/.worktrees/&lt;name&gt;</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each session gets a scratch folder, <code>/var/tmp/pulsar-agents/&lt;name&gt;</code>, in{' '}
    <code>$PULSAR_SCRATCH</code>. Pulsar’s guide tells agents to put VM disks, builds and downloads
    there. It’s deleted with the session.
  </p>
  <p>
    When an agent in a session you aren’t looking at finishes or needs you, a notification says so.
    Claude Code, Codex and Antigravity CLI tell Pulsar when they finish a turn, and the session
    shows as waiting until you send it something. opencode doesn’t yet, and notifies you only when
    it rings the terminal bell.
  </p>
  <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" language="bash" filename="undo" code={UNDO}></arc-code-block>
  <p>
    Undo works in git repositories. It puts the files back as they were when the session first
    started (or last started with <code>--new</code>), however often it restarted since, and deletes
    files added since. Files git ignores are left as they are, and so is any file the undo couldn’t
    take back, which it names. Branches, and commits made since, stay where they are. A whole undo
    refuses to run while a session is running in that checkout, and <code>--file</code> doesn’t.
  </p>
  <p>
    On a fresh install <a href="#sandbox">the sandbox</a> and <a href="#guard">the guard</a> are
    both off. A session you leave running can read your whole home folder, and anything it runs can
    layer packages or roll back without a password.
  </p>

  <h2 id="disk">Disk space</h2>
  <p>
    The footer of the app’s session list shows how much room is left on <code>/var</code>, amber
    when it’s getting tight and red when it’s nearly full. Click it for the big things in{' '}
    <code>/var/tmp</code>, <code>~/.cache</code> and the sessions’ scratch folders, each with
    Delete.
  </p>
  <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" language="bash" filename="disk" code={DISK}></arc-code-block>
  <p>
    On the NVIDIA image, the driver saves the GPU’s memory to <code>/var/lib/systemd/sleep</code>{' '}
    as the machine suspends. Without room for it, the suspend fails and the machine stays awake.
  </p>

  <h2 id="messages">Sessions talking to each other</h2>
  <p>
    One session can read another’s screen and leave it a message, and a long command can run as a
    job that tells a session how it ended. Agents use the same through <code>pulsar mcp</code>.
  </p>
  <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" language="bash" filename="messages" code={MESSAGES}></arc-code-block>
  <ul>
    <li>
      A message goes in only once that agent has ended its turn, never into a permission question,
      and while you’re watching the session only with <code>--now</code>. It’s labelled with the
      sender (<code>&lt;name&gt;@&lt;machine&gt;</code> from another of your machines).
    </li>
    <li>
      Claude Code gets its messages through its own hooks, at the end of a turn or while it sits
      idle, so nothing is typed into it. Antigravity CLI gets them the same way at the end of a
      turn. Codex, and an idle Antigravity CLI, get them typed into the prompt.
    </li>
    <li>
      opencode takes no messages, because Pulsar can’t tell when its turns end. Sandboxed sessions
      don’t take them yet.
    </li>
    <li>
      Every message is logged in <code>~/.local/state/pulsar/agents/messages.log</code>. One session
      can send another at most 10 in 10 minutes. Preferences in the Agents app, or{' '}
      <code>pulsar agent messages</code>, turns messages off or changes that limit.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A screen read from another session holds whatever its agent printed, so an agent reading it is
    reading untrusted text. The sender’s name is what the sending session says it is: an agent
    with a shell can claim any name.
  </p>
  <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" language="bash" filename="jobs" code={JOBS}></arc-code-block>
  <p>
    A job runs as you, outside any sandbox, so a sandboxed agent can’t start one.
  </p>

  <h2 id="machines">Your other machines</h2>
  <p>
    <code>pulsar machine</code> hands work to your other Pulsar machines over SSH. From the laptop
    you can start an agent on the desktop, run a long job there, and bring the branch back.
  </p>
  <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" language="bash" filename="machine" code={MACHINE}></arc-code-block>
  <p>
    <code>run</code> and <code>job</code> send your branch by git over SSH, with what isn’t
    committed as one extra commit, to the same project path in your home folder there. The agent
    starts in a worktree on a new branch named for the session, as you, with that machine’s logins
    and sandbox setting. <code>--notify work</code> tells the session <code>work</code> here how
    the job ended. <code>fetch</code> writes only <code>desktop/train</code> and never touches your
    checkout. A branch that already exists there is refused, not overwritten.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>New files git doesn’t ignore are sent too</strong>, so an untracked <code>.env</code>{' '}
    that isn’t in <code>.gitignore</code> goes with the branch. Files git ignores stay here.
  </p>
  <p>
    In the app, sessions on other machines are listed as <code>&lt;name&gt;@&lt;machine&gt;</code>{' '}
    under “&lt;project&gt; · &lt;machine&gt;” and open like local ones. A machine that doesn’t
    answer keeps its sessions in the list, dimmed, with <strong>Try Again</strong>. Agents get the
    MCP tools <code>machines</code>, <code>machine_run</code>, <code>machine_job</code> and{' '}
    <code>machine_fetch</code>, and ask you before using the last three.
  </p>
  <h3>Before you add one</h3>
  <p>
    Pulsar runs ssh with no prompts, so SSH has to be on on the other machine (Pulsar doesn’t turn
    it on) and your key has to be there already:
  </p>
  <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" language="bash" filename="once" code={SSH_SETUP}></arc-code-block>
  <p>
    A key that asks for a passphrase fails. If your SSH agent holds many keys (1Password’s does),
    set <code>IdentityFile</code> and <code>IdentitiesOnly</code> for that host in{' '}
    <code>~/.ssh/config</code>, or sshd refuses after too many tries.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Announce This Machine</strong>, in the app’s Preferences under Your Machines (or{' '}
    <code>pulsar machine announce on</code>), is off until you turn it on. It publishes your user
    name, the host name, the Pulsar release and whether SSH is on to everyone on the local
    network. It opens nothing. Your other machines then list it with <strong>Add</strong>, and{' '}
    <code>pulsar machine discover</code> shows it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The other machine needs Pulsar 26.11.2 or newer. Handing off runs and jobs, and fetching them
    back, has been tested against one real second machine. Announcing, discovering, and messages
    between machines have only been tested against stand-ins so far. A Claude Code session started
    in a project that’s new to that machine stops at Claude’s question about trusting the folder,
    so open it in the app to answer.
  </p>

  <h2 id="model">A model on your own GPU</h2>
  <p>opencode can use a local OpenAI-compatible server, with no account.</p>
  <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" language="bash" filename="model" code={MODEL}></arc-code-block>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Runs llama.cpp’s server image as a rootless user service: CUDA on the NVIDIA image, Vulkan
      with a GPU at <code>/dev/dri</code>, else CPU.
    </li>
    <li>First start downloads the model into <code>~/.local/share/pulsar/models</code>.</li>
    <li>
      Serves <code>http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1</code>, local only. Requests need the key in{' '}
      <code>~/.config/pulsar/model-key</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      In opencode, pick “Local (Pulsar)” with <code>/models</code>. <code>pulsar agent model</code>{' '}
      also prints the command for aider.
    </li>
    <li>
      Stops at logout unless started with <code>--at-login</code> (a loaded model holds gigabytes of
      video memory).
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="sandbox">The sandbox</h2>
  <p>
    The agent runs in a rootless container that sees only the project you started it in, its own
    login, your git identity and its session’s scratch folder, with no SSH agent and no session
    bus. Pushes go through a gate on
    the host that pushes with your credentials and refuses force-pushes, deletions and tags.
  </p>
  <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" language="bash" filename="sandbox" code={SANDBOX}></arc-code-block>
  <ul>
    <li>
      The agent can commit, but git config and hooks (the repo’s, its submodules’, and any
      in-project hooks path or include) are read-only, and <code>.git</code> can’t be moved.
    </li>
    <li>
      A new <code>commondir</code> or <code>.git</code> in the tree is renamed to{' '}
      <code>&lt;name&gt;.from-sandbox</code> when the session ends. Until then, an editor running
      git in the background could follow it: close the editor on the project while an untrusted
      agent works.
    </li>
    <li>
      A linked worktree inside its main checkout, where <code>--worktree</code> puts them, works. One
      outside it, and a submodule checkout, are refused, because their git directory is outside the
      project where the sandbox can’t protect it.
    </li>
    <li>
      A session in its own worktree sees only that worktree and the repository’s <code>.git</code>,
      not the main checkout or other sessions’ worktrees.
    </li>
    <li>
      The project’s own agent settings (<code>.claude/</code>, <code>.mcp.json</code>,{' '}
      <code>.codex/</code>, <code>.gemini/</code>, <code>.agents/</code>, <code>.opencode/</code>,{' '}
      <code>opencode.json</code>) are read-only, and one the agent creates is renamed to{' '}
      <code>&lt;name&gt;.from-sandbox</code> when the session ends. Antigravity CLI reads hooks and
      MCP servers from <code>.agents/</code>.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Antigravity CLI and opencode 2 sign in again inside the sandbox, because their logins (the
    keyring, and opencode’s <code>opencode.db</code>) aren’t copied in. Signing in to Antigravity
    CLI in the sandbox hasn’t been tested yet.
  </p>
  <p>Type the agent’s name as usual:</p>
  <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" filename="claude" code={SANDBOX_START}></arc-code-block>
  <p>
    Start it inside a project. It refuses your whole home folder. In interactive bash,{' '}
    <code>claude</code>, <code>codex</code>, <code>agy</code> and <code>opencode</code> (and{' '}
    <code>gemini</code> and <code>aider</code>) go through the sandbox, and{' '}
    <code>pulsar agent sandbox wrap off</code> stops that. Scripts, other shells and <code>command claude</code> don’t.{' '}
    <code>pulsar agent run claude</code> always follows your settings.
  </p>
  <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" language="bash" filename="more" code={SANDBOX_MORE}></arc-code-block>
  <p>A repository can require the sandbox (this file can only tighten settings):</p>
  <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" filename=".pulsar/agent.toml" code={REPO_TOML}></arc-code-block>
  <p class="hint">
    The sandbox leaves the network open. The rest of what it lets in and out is in{' '}
    <a href="/docs/agents-safety#the-sandbox-and-a-push-that-never-holds-your-key">Agents and safety</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="guard">Guard and checkpoints</h2>
  <p>
    On stock Fedora, and on Pulsar until you turn the guard on, an admin’s session and anything it
    starts can layer packages, roll back, clean up deployments, and install or remove system
    Flatpaks without a password.
  </p>
  <arc-code-block class="theme-fixed-dark" filename="pulsar agent guard" code={GUARD}></arc-code-block>
  <p>
    <code>sudo pulsar agent guard on</code> makes all five ask for the admin password. Updates stay
    ungated. <code>sudo pulsar agent guard off</code> restores Fedora’s default.
  </p>
  <p>
    Take a checkpoint before a long session with <code>sudo pulsar checkpoint "before the agent"</code>.
    Afterwards, <code>diff</code> shows what changed and <code>restore</code> puts it back. More in{' '}
    <a href="/docs/updates#checkpoint">Checkpoints</a>.
  </p>
</Docs>
